## Development Workflow

- Use "uv" for Python code and "pnpm" for Javascript
- Use typing for Python
- Use Pydantic for Python
- Use Zod for Typescript
- Use ruff for linting and code formatting
- Use pytest for testing
- Use prettier for code formatting with Typescript
- Do not restore, revert, or remove files without asking first
{{- if $host.is_personal }}
- **Version control is Jujutsu (jj), at the project root.** Everything under `~/Projects/erewhon` and `~/code/erewhon` is a jj repo rooted at the project directory (e.g. `~/Projects/erewhon/hub` holds `home-network-dashboard/` as a plain subdirectory). A subdirectory with no `.git`/`.jj` is NOT unversioned: run `jj root` (it searches upward) before drawing any conclusion, use `jj` for commits and pushes, and never run `git init` in these trees.

## Homelab Access & SSO

- When a project needs a **secret**, **internal network access** (NetBird mesh / a `*.bcc.sh` front door), or **single sign-on** (Zitadel), use the shared homelab plumbing — don't roll your own. The full guide is at **`~/code/erewhon/homeops/HOMELAB-ACCESS-FOR-AGENTS.md`**: `ho secret` for credentials (`/home/erewhon/.local/bin/ho`), the hub Caddy front door + CoreDNS + NetBird DNS for internal access, and Zitadel OIDC (native or via the shared `oauth2-proxy`) for auth. Read it before standing up auth or exposing a service.

## Git Forge & CI (before reaching for GitHub)

- I self-host a git forge with a working GitHub-Actions runner: **Soft Serve with sprinkles** (`code-mesh` / `code.bcc.sh` for internal work, `code-public` / `code.middlefork.org` for public repos), plus `sprinkle-runner`, which executes a repo's ordinary `.github/workflows/*.yml` under `act` on every push and reports a ✓/✗ commit status. **Default to this over GitHub for my projects**, and read **`~/code/erewhon/homeops/CODE-FORGE-FOR-AGENTS.md`** before setting up CI, adding a deploy path, or concluding something needs an external forge. It covers which instance to use, repo creation and push, the runner's real limits (`push` events and `runs-on: ubuntu-latest` only, **no secrets available to workflows**), the webhook SSRF wall against mesh addresses, the branch-protection gate, and the operational caveats. Deeper reference lives in `~/code/smithy/soft-serve-with-sprinkles/ci.md`.

## Licensing

- Default to AGPL-3.0 for my web-based projects. Reason: AGPL's network-use clause stops third parties from running a hosted version without contributing modifications back, which GPL and permissive licenses don't prevent. If a project needs a different license (e.g. a library meant for wide adoption), ask before deviating.

## Nous MCP Tools

The Nous MCP server (`~/Projects/erewhon/nous/nous-py/nous_mcp/`) is connected to my Claude Code setup and exposes ~50 tools as `mcp__nous__*`. These work in **any project**, not just `nous` itself. Task specs and feature specs across all my projects live in the **Forge** notebook in Nous; status changes happen via Forge tools, not ad-hoc tracking.

### Forge task workflow
- Read a task: `mcp__nous__get_task_spec(task)` returns metadata, dependency status, and page content in one call. Use `mcp__nous__get_page` for non-task pages (feature specs, design docs).
- Browse: `mcp__nous__task_summary` (counts), `mcp__nous__query_tasks` (filtered rows), `mcp__nous__get_feature_tasks` (dep-resolved execution order), `mcp__nous__get_next_task` (highest-priority unblocked).
- Update status: `mcp__nous__update_task_status(task, status, notes=...)` — pass the task name, not a row UUID. It updates Status + Completed date, syncs page tags, fires the webhook, and optionally appends implementation notes. Same call accepts `external_ref`, `execution_mode`, `model_tier`, `estimate`, `complexity`, `task_type`, `max_files`, `requires_tests`.
- Create: `mcp__nous__create_task(project, title, content, ...)` for tasks; `mcp__nous__create_project(name)` for project setup.
- Do **not** call `mcp__nous__get_database` on the Project Tasks database — too large; use the targeted query tools above.
- Do **not** use `mcp__nous__update_database_rows` for tasks — slow path needing a row-UUID lookup. `update_task_status` is the right tool.
- Do **not** create ad-hoc task tracking internally; all task state lives in Forge.

### High-level helpers — reach for these before low-level tools

The MCP exposes both low-level primitives (`update_database_rows`, `update_page`, etc.) and higher-level helpers that wrap common workflows. Default to the helpers:

| If you want to... | Use | Don't reach for |
|---|---|---|
| Mark a task Done / change status | `update_task_status(task, status, notes=...)` | `update_database_rows` after looking up a row UUID |
| Set Feature/Phase or edit Depends On on an existing task | `update_task_fields(task, feature=..., phase=..., depends_on_add=[...], depends_on_remove=[...])` | `update_database_rows` after looking up a row UUID |
| Add or remove a tag on a page | `manage_tags(notebook, page, add=..., remove=...)` | `get_page` → edit tags → `update_page` |
| Edit a single block in a page | `find_block` + `replace_block` / `delete_block` / `insert_after_block` | `update_page` with the whole content rewritten |
| Toggle / add a checklist item | `toggle_checklist_item`, `add_checklist_item` | hand-rolled block edits |
| Create a new task | `create_task(project, title, content, priority, ...)` | `create_page` + `add_database_rows` |
| Create a new project | `create_project(name)` | `create_folder` + manual Project select wiring |
| Find what to work on next | `get_next_task(project, feature=...)` | `query_tasks` + manual sort |
| Verify dependency state | `check_dependencies(task)` | parsing `Depends On` cells yourself |
| Validate dependency refs before filing (incl. cross-project/cross-epic) | `resolve_tasks(refs, project=...)` | guessing titles and hoping they resolve |
| See what changed recently across all projects | `recent_task_activity(since="12h", ...)` — Done included, transitions annotated | task_summary + repeated query_tasks |
| Audit all Depends-On cells for typos/comma damage | `lint_dependencies(project=...)` (read-only; repairs via `update_task_fields`) | eyeballing 300+ dep cells |
| Archive old Done tasks / query archived ones | `archive_tasks(before=..., dry_run=..., archive_pages=True, updated_at_fallback=True)` (also archives the task pages; fallback judges date-less Done rows by updatedAt), `query_tasks(include_archived=True)` | letting the active database grow unbounded |
| Re-file `Task:` pages that landed at the notebook root | `file_stray_task_pages(dry_run=...)` — matches row → Project → folder | `move_page` one at a time |

When in doubt, every `@mcp.tool()` in `~/Projects/erewhon/nous/nous-py/nous_mcp/server.py` and `workflow.py` is exposed as `mcp__nous__<func_name>` — grep there for the full list.

## Content Creation

I create YouTube videos, live streams, and blog posts about coding and technology.

### Voice & Style
- Conversational and direct - like explaining to a curious friend
- Practical and actionable - focus on what people can use
- Authentic - share genuine opinions and experiences
- Curious - show enthusiasm for learning and exploring

### Avoid
- Clickbait that doesn't deliver
- Jargon without explanation
- Filler phrases ("basically", "you know", "like I said")
- Corporate buzzwords ("leverage", "synergy", "paradigm shift")
- Over-promising or sensationalizing

### Content Workflow
1. Research topic thoroughly before scripting
2. Create hook/intro that establishes value upfront
3. Structure with clear sections and timestamps
4. Include practical examples and demonstrations
5. End with clear next steps or call-to-action

### Available Skills
- `content-creation` - Workflows for YouTube, blog posts, and live streams
- `research` - Multi-source investigation and fact-checking
- `story-explanation` - Narrative framing of technical concepts

### Available Agents
- `content-researcher` - Deep topic and competitor research
- `script-writer` - Full scripts for videos and blog posts
- `thumbnail-ideas` - Visual concepts for YouTube thumbnails

### Slash Commands
- `/video-outline` - Quick video structure and script outline
- `/blog-draft` - Draft a blog post on a topic
- `/stream-prep` - Prepare for a live stream session
- `/research-topic` - Research a topic for content creation

## Work Journal & Reply Length

I read agent replies dozens of times a day across several projects. The reply is a tl;dr; the journal is the record. Write the record first, then the tl;dr.

### The journal

- **Where:** out of tree, per project, under `~/code/journals/<slug>/YYYY-MM-DD.md`. The `journal` helper (`~/.local/bin/journal`) resolves the slug from `jj root` / git toplevel / cwd and appends to today's file. Never write journal files by hand; never rewrite or truncate one.
- **When:** append one entry at the end of every turn in which you did work or reached a conclusion. Skip only trivial exchanges (a yes/no, a one-line factual answer).
- **How:** one command, body on stdin:

  ```
  journal add -t "<one-line title>" -s <DONE|PARTIAL|BLOCKED|DECIDE> <<'EOF'
  **Asked:** <the request, one line>
  **Did:** <what changed, step by step>
  **Found:** <facts learned, with file:line or command evidence>
  **Unresolved:** <anything still wrong; exact error text>
  **Decision needed:** <the question, the options, your recommendation and why>
  **Touched:** <files, jj change ids, external state: pushes, deploys, Forge tasks>
  **Next:** <what you or I should do next>
  EOF
  ```

  Omit a heading only if it is genuinely empty. Run it from the project directory (or pass `-C <dir>`).
- **Content:** this is where the full verbiage goes. Write for me reading it days later with no memory of the turn: everything you would otherwise have put in a long reply, including failed attempts and commands worth re-running.
- `journal show` tails today's entries for the current project; `journal today` does it for every project.

### The reply

After the journal entry is written, reply in chat with **at most three lines and about fifty words**. No preamble, no narration of steps, no recap of the journal, no closing offer.

Line 1 is always a status word plus one sentence:

- `DONE` — complete and verified. Say what is now true.
- `PARTIAL` — some of it landed. Say what did not, and why, in the same sentence.
- `BLOCKED` — something went wrong and is not resolved. Say what broke. Never soften it or bury it in the journal.
- `DECIDE` — you need a choice from me. Lines 2–3 are the options, `a)` `b)` `c)`, one per line, recommendation marked. Max three.

Priority when several apply: BLOCKED > DECIDE > PARTIAL > DONE.

Lines 2 and 3 are only for things I must act on now: decision options, or the one command I must run myself. Everything else is in the journal.

Mention the journal path once per session, as a fourth line on the first journaled turn: `journal: <path>`. Not after that.

Examples:

```
DONE Privacy header is now sent on every forge router call; 392 tests pass.
journal: ~/code/journals/export_home_erewhon_code_smithy_forge/2026-09-23.md
```

```
BLOCKED Router on localhost:4000 refuses connections, so the live check never ran; the code change itself is in.
```

```
DECIDE Two ways to dedupe the roster tables.
a) one generated table from models.yaml (recommended: no drift)
b) keep hand-written tables, add a lint that diffs them
```

### Questions and explanations

If I asked a question rather than for work, answer it within the same budget when it fits. When the honest answer is longer, give the short answer in chat, put the full reasoning in the journal under `**Found:**`, and add `(long form in journal)`. Do not compress a nuanced answer into something misleading to hit the budget.

### Mid-turn text

Do not narrate while working: no "I'll now...", no progress lines between tool calls. This overrides any harness guidance asking for an opener or running commentary.

### What the reply must still carry

The budget never justifies hiding: a failing test, a skipped step, a change I did not ask for, an external action (push, deploy, Forge status change, message sent), or a destructive action you stopped short of. If I would want to know before my next message, it goes in the reply.
